<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Turduli</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 150 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A people of the south-west, close kin of the Turdetani, with a northern branch, the Turduli Veteres, living near the Douro in Portugal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Turduli lived in the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> regions next to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/turdetani">Turdetani</a>, from whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> said they could hardly be distinguished. A separate group, the Turduli Veteres or Old Turduli, settled south of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> in northern Portugal, probably after migrating from the south.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is recorded of their government. Their economy relied on farming, herding and the mineral wealth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>. Pliny lists their towns in both <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-baetica">Baetica</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania</a>, which shows how widespread the group was.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>They were absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Roman Hispania</a> Ulterior during the 2nd century BCE, following the conquest of the south after 206 BCE and the Lusitanian wars. Under Augustus their lands were divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-baetica">Baetica</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lusitania">Lusitania</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/turduli.jpg" alt="Turduli" width="320" height="297"><figcaption>Image: Ángel M. Felicísimo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Novillo_de_Porcuna_-_%C3%81ngel_M._Felic%C3%ADsimo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 150 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turdulians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-325&amp;focus=turduli" aria-label="Turduli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4611 718 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Turduli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 974h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4931 974h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Spain. <a href="/atlas?year=-325&amp;focus=turduli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
